<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # School Days – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> School Days</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Yesterday Was Wednesday.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Identify independently common actions seen in daily routines. Interactive: Participate in simple formulaic question-and-answer exchanges on familiar subjects.</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can identify common actions using simple phrases (e.g., "I write," "I read," and "I listen."). Can participate in simple question-and-answer exchanges about days of the week (e.g., "What day is it today?" "Today is Tuesday." "What day is tomorrow?" "Tomorrow is Wednesday."). Can describe common actions in daily routines in simple exchanges. (e.g., "We clean up on Monday." "We sing on Tuesday." "I read on Wednesday.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and leads a "Days of the Week" song, pointing to a large classroom calendar as each day is sung. The teacher then uses large flashcards for days of the week (Monday-Friday) and action verbs (read, write, listen, draw, talk, clean up) and asks students to repeat chorally. The teacher then asks, "What day is today?" and elicits "Today is [Day]." The teacher then asks, "Yesterday was...?" and students respond chorally, "Yesterday was [Day]."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard for "Wednesday" and an action flashcard "read." The teacher says clearly, "Yesterday was Wednesday. I read on Wednesday." The teacher models the pronunciation of /w/ in "Wednesday" and /f/ in "Friday" by exaggerating the sounds. The teacher then says, "Today is Thursday. We draw on Thursday!" The teacher models the full sentences and encourages choral repetition.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding using simple yes/no questions and gestures.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "Is today Tuesday?" (Students respond: "No!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "Was yesterday Friday?" (Students respond: "No!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: (Holding "read" flashcard) "Do we read on Wednesday?" (Students respond: "Yes!")</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">[5-7 min]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "School Days".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a large, colorful "Our Class Schedule" poster for the week (Monday-Friday) with simple illustrations for each day's main activity (e.g., Monday: Math/count, Tuesday: English/say, Wednesday: Reading/read, Thursday: Science/learn, Friday: Art/draw). Each day clearly states the day and the activity verb with an image. The poster uses target vocabulary: Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday, math, English, science, read, write, draw, learn, talk.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Sociolinguistic: Communicating about daily classroom activities and schedules using appropriate language and turn-taking (e.g., "Today is Monday.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher points to a day on the schedule and asks, "What day is this?" (Students answer chorally: "Monday!") The teacher then points to the activity for that day and asks, "What do we do on Monday?" (Students answer chorally: "We do Math!" or "We count!") The teacher repeats this for several days, reinforcing the days and corresponding activities. The teacher emphasizes the /w/ in "Wednesday" and /f/ in "Friday" during these interactions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">[8-10 min]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Grammar: Past simple with the verb "be" (e.g., "Yesterday was..."), Present simple (e.g., "Today is..."), WH-questions ("What is it?"). Vocabulary: Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday, read, write, draw, listen, say. Pronunciation: /w/ in "Wednesday", /f/ in "Friday". Pragmatic: Answering questions about days: "Yesterday was Wednesday."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice (Choral Repetition):</b> The teacher uses large flashcards for days of the week and a selection of action verbs (read, write, draw, listen). The teacher shows a day card, e.g., "Tuesday," then an action card, e.g., "listen." The teacher models: "Today is Tuesday. We listen on Tuesday." Students repeat chorally. The teacher then shows "Monday" and "write," modeling "Yesterday was Monday. We write on Monday." Students repeat. This is repeated for several combinations, ensuring strong, clear choral pronunciation of /w/ and /f/.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice (Teacher-led Q&A):</b> The teacher asks the whole class, "What day is it today?" Students respond, "Today is [Day]." The teacher then asks, "What day was yesterday?" Students respond, "Yesterday was [Day]." Next, the teacher points to the class schedule poster and asks, "What do we do on [Day]?" (e.g., "What do we do on Wednesday?"). Students answer using the schedule, "We read on Wednesday." The teacher encourages full sentence responses and corrects pronunciation, particularly for /w/ and /f/.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction (Sentence Frames):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a small set of day cards (Monday-Friday) and activity picture cards (read, write, draw, listen, say). Student A picks a day card and an activity card and asks Student B, "What do we do on [Day]?" or "What did we do yesterday on [Day]?" (if referring to the previous day). Student B looks at their cards or thinks of an activity and responds using the sentence frame: "On [Day], we [activity]." The teacher circulates, provides support, and monitors for correct use of "is" and "was," target vocabulary, and pronunciation. They then switch roles.</li>
                </ol></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">[15-18 min]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "School Days".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task:</b> "Our Daily Actions Share." Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of five small cards, one for each school day (Monday-Friday), with space to draw a simple activity they *do* or *like to do* on that day (e.g., draw for Art, book for Reading, numbers for Math). They also get a "Today" and "Yesterday" card. Students will talk to their partner about two days: what they do *today* and what they did *yesterday*, using the target grammar and vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students first draw one activity on each of their day cards that they like to do or typically do at school (e.g., "I draw on Friday").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">In pairs, Student A starts by holding up the "Today" card and saying, "Today is [current day]. On [current day], we [activity from their card]."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Then, Student A holds up the "Yesterday" card and says, "Yesterday was [previous day]. On [previous day], we [activity from their card]."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B listens and then takes their turn, describing their "Today" and "Yesterday" activities.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher encourages students to express preferences if they wish, e.g., "I like Friday. We draw on Friday!" The teacher monitors for fluency, turn-taking, and active use of target structures.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> This activity directly connects to "School Days" and uses the structure "Yesterday Was Wednesday" (or whatever the previous day was) to describe daily actions within the school context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">[10-12 min]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-assessment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> The teacher uses an observation checklist during the Performance stage, noting student pairs for their ability to:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use "Today is..." and "Yesterday was..." correctly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identify common actions using simple phrases (e.g., "I read," "We draw").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronounce days of the week intelligibly, especially /w/ and /f/.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Participate in simple Q&A exchanges with a partner.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student will briefly share with the class (or with their pair, then one or two pairs share with the class) one sentence about "Today" and one about "Yesterday" based on their activity cards. For example: "Today is Thursday. We draw on Thursday!" "Yesterday was Wednesday. I read a book."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of /w/ in "Wednesday" and /f/ in "Friday".</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of "Today is..." and "Yesterday was...".</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of at least two target vocabulary verbs (e.g., read, draw, write).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of communication in simple sentences.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick "thumbs up/down" or simple drawing on their paper:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I say 'Today is...' and 'Yesterday was...'?" (Happy face / Sad face)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I talk about what we do at school?" (Happy face / Sad face)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">[5-7 min]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was easy to say today about the days of the week?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was a little difficult to say about what we do on different days?</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The students showed good enthusiasm during the pair work and were generally able to produce the target sentences "Today is..." and "Yesterday was..." with varying degrees of accuracy. The pronunciation of /w/ in "Wednesday" was challenging for some, requiring more focused drilling. The visual schedule and action flashcards were very helpful for comprehension and production. Some students still confused "read" and "write" when speaking. More repetition on distinguishing these actions orally is needed. Adjustments: For next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing), I will ensure to visually reinforce the written forms of "read" and "write" clearly and provide sentence starters to scaffold the writing task, building on the oral production from today.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> The next lesson will focus on Writing, where students will apply the vocabulary and grammatical structures ("Today is...", "Yesterday was...") to write simple sentences about their daily school activities based on the oral practice from this lesson. They will specifically practice writing about what they do on different school days.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">[3-5 min]</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>